<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document" style="font-size:144%;">Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span><span id="id4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualcomm AI Research is an initiative of Qualcomm Technologies, Inc.</span></span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Evgenii Egorov
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Work done during an internship in Qualcomm AI Research.˜Alt. email: <span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:56%;">email.evgenii.egorov@gmail.com</span></span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Amsterdam
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">egorov.evgenyy@ya.ru</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hanno Ackermann
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Markus Nagel
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hong Cai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Qualcomm AI Research
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">hackerma, markusn, hongcai @qti.qualcomm.com</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Attention layers apply a sequence-to-sequence mapping whose parameters depend on the pairwise interactions of the input elements.
However, without any structural assumptions, memory and compute scale quadratically with the sequence length. The two main ways to mitigate this are to introduce sparsity by ignoring a sufficient amount of pairwise interactions or to introduce recurrent dependence along them, as SSM does. Although both approaches are reasonable, they both have disadvantages.
We propose a novel algorithm that combines the advantages of both concepts. Our idea is based on the efficient inversion of tree-structured matrices.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modeling interactions in high-dimensional objects efficiently has been a long-standing challenge in machine learning, particularly when short-range dependencies are insufficient and long-range interactions must be captured. The Transformer architecture <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> was a breakthrough in addressing long-range dependencies, but it suffers from quadratic computational complexity with respect to input length. To mitigate this, numerous efficient Transformer variants have been proposed, exploiting sparsity or hierarchical structure. Examples include BigBird <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Longformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, Performer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and Swin Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, each designed to handle longer sequences or high-dimensional inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative approach is offered by State-Space Models (SSMs), which recast sequence modeling as linear dynamical systems, allowing exact or approximate recurrent computation over long sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. SSMs have been extended to multidimensional signals, audio and video tasks, demonstrating strong performance while maintaining linear complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Variants like S4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and S5<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> further improve parameterization and initialization, leading to robust training and effective long-range dependency modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on advances in graphical models and deep learning architectures, we introduce a new structural state-space layer, Myo, illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 The Myosotis (Myo) Layer ‣ Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Myo retains the linear complexity of existing SSM layers but explicitly leverages structural assumptions about token interactions, connecting sparsity patterns with recurrence constraints via matrix inversion. This framework allows SSM layers to emerge as a special case and enables direct inheritance of initialization schemes from the SSM literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. The layer is straightforward to implement (see Sec. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Appendix: Details of forward pass computation ‣ Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) and can incorporate domain knowledge in the form of graphs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review two related sequence-to-sequence layers: the state space model layer (SSM) and self-attention. As our goal is computational complexity, we focus on the core components of the layer and ignore mapping from input sequence to the parameters of the layer. We identify a gap between two approaches that motivates our solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We denote tuples of length <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, when the length is clear. If all elements of the sequence belong to the same space <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, then we write <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in V^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in V^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, otherwise specify per element <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}\in V_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}\in V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The first index of sequence is <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote the input sequence of the layer as a tuple <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times M}</annotation></semantics></math> and the output sequence as a tuple <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We index elements of a tuple with lower index, for example, <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{M},x_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{M},x_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th and the <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th elements of the sequences <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both self-attention and state space layers are parametrized of the sequence-to-sequence mapping <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:\mathbb{R}^{L\times M}\to\mathbb{R}^{L\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:\mathbb{R}^{L\times M}\to\mathbb{R}^{L\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, which itself can be input dependent. We refer to its matrix as the kernel matrix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>State Space Model (SSM) Layer</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter sequences <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A state space layer maps an input sequence to the output <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> by the following recurrence:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=B_{1}u_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=B_{1}u_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1X.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A_{k-1}x_{k-1}+B_{k}u_{k}\quad\mbox{for}\;k\in\{2,\dots,L\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext>for</mtext><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A_{k-1}x_{k-1}+B_{k}u_{k}\quad\mbox{for}\;k\in\{2,\dots,L\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As matrix multiplication is an associative operation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((AB)(CD)=((A(B(C(D))))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((AB)(CD)=((A(B(C(D))))</annotation></semantics></math>, the above recurrence can be computed in parallel by reusing intermediate computations using <span id="S2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">associative scan</span> operations. As differentiation is a linear operation, the same is applicable for differentiation through the associative scan on a backward pass. The time complexity on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> processors is <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O((L/T+\log_{2}T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O((L/T+\log_{2}T)</annotation></semantics></math> and the space complexity is linear over the sequence length <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Attention Layer</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are given the input sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> and parameter sequences <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{1:L},k_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1:L},k_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and a nonlinearity <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. We call the composition of the non-linearity and inner product the kernel <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,y)=\sigma(\langle x,y\rangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,y)=\sigma(\langle x,y\rangle)</annotation></semantics></math> of the attention. The attention layer maps the input sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1:L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:L}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E2X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k\in\{1,\dots,L\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k\in\{1,\dots,L\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2X.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}=\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot\dfrac{\sigma(\langle q_{k},k_{n}\rangle)}{\sum_{n^{\prime}\in\{1,\dots,L\}}\sigma(\langle q_{k},k_{n^{\prime}}\rangle)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}=\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot\dfrac{\sigma(\langle q_{k},k_{n}\rangle)}{\sum_{n^{\prime}\in\{1,\dots,L\}}\sigma(\langle q_{k},k_{n^{\prime}}\rangle)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, the complexity over both time and space of the self-attention layer is quadratic with respect to the length of the sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. To reduce it, additional assumptions about a function <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> should be made. Two common approaches are the separability and sparsity assumptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Separability assumption</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following parameterization of a fixed or learnable dictionary of functions <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{p}\}_{p=1}^{P},\phi_{p}:\mathbb{R}^{N}\to\mathbb{R}^{N_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{p}\}_{p=1}^{P},\phi_{p}:\mathbb{R}^{N}\to\mathbb{R}^{N_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using it, we define a separable kernel:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k(x,y)=\sum_{p=1}^{P}\langle\phi_{p}(x),\phi_{p}(y)\rangle,x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k(x,y)=\sum_{p=1}^{P}\langle\phi_{p}(x),\phi_{p}(y)\rangle,x,y\in\mathbb{R}^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">As <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{p=1}^{P}\langle\phi_{p}(x),\phi_{p}(y)\rangle=\langle(\phi_{1}(x),\dots,\phi_{P}(x)),(\phi_{1}(y),\dots,\phi_{P}(y))\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{p=1}^{P}\langle\phi_{p}(x),\phi_{p}(y)\rangle=\langle(\phi_{1}(x),\dots,\phi_{P}(x)),(\phi_{1}(y),\dots,\phi_{P}(y))\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider the feature stacking map <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi:\mathbb{R}^{N}\to\mathbb{R}^{\sum_{n=1}^{P}N_{p}},\psi(x)=(\phi_{1}(x),\dots,\phi_{P}(x)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:\mathbb{R}^{N}\to\mathbb{R}^{\sum_{n=1}^{P}N_{p}},\psi(x)=(\phi_{1}(x),\dots,\phi_{P}(x)))</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the "linear" self-attention map:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}=\psi(q_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{D},\;\;K_{k}=\psi(k_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{D},\;\;D=\sum_{n\in\{1,\dots,P\}}N_{p}\;\;\mbox{for}\;\;k\in\{1,\dots,L\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}=\psi(q_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{D},\;\;K_{k}=\psi(k_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{D},\;\;D=\sum_{n\in\{1,\dots,P\}}N_{p}\;\;\mbox{for}\;\;k\in\{1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{k}=\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot\dfrac{\langle Q_{k},K_{n}\rangle}{\sum_{n^{\prime}=1}^{L}\langle Q_{k},K_{n^{\prime}}\rangle}=(\langle Q_{k},\sum_{n^{\prime}\in\{1,\dots,L\}}K_{n^{\prime}}\rangle)^{-1}\sum_{p=1}^{D}\left(\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot(K_{n})_{p}\right)(Q_{k})_{p}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{k}=\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot\dfrac{\langle Q_{k},K_{n}\rangle}{\sum_{n^{\prime}=1}^{L}\langle Q_{k},K_{n^{\prime}}\rangle}=(\langle Q_{k},\sum_{n^{\prime}\in\{1,\dots,L\}}K_{n^{\prime}}\rangle)^{-1}\sum_{p=1}^{D}\left(\sum_{n=1}^{L}u_{n}\cdot(K_{n})_{p}\right)(Q_{k})_{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As a result, for fixed <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll L</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a desirable linear complexity solution over the length of the sequence. If we keep only the lower triangular part (<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq k)</annotation></semantics></math>, the relation to the state-space model can be made more explicit. Consider <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> before scaling and summating over <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and denote it by <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{p}_{k}=q^{p}_{k}\sum_{n\leq k}u_{n}\cdot k^{p}_{n}=\frac{q_{k}^{P}}{q_{k-1}^{p}}x^{p}_{k-1}+(k^{p}_{k}q_{k}^{p})u_{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{p}_{k}=q^{p}_{k}\sum_{n\leq k}u_{n}\cdot k^{p}_{n}=\frac{q_{k}^{P}}{q_{k-1}^{p}}x^{p}_{k-1}+(k^{p}_{k}q_{k}^{p})u_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which reassembles the recurrence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the separability assumption on the kernel reduces the number of free parameters of the kernel matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\times D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\times D</annotation></semantics></math>. Although the kernel matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is generally dense, this reduces computational costs from quadratic to linear over the sequence length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sparsity assumptions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another way to reduce complexity is to introduce a structural sparsity pattern in the attention kernel computation. It is convenient to represent a sparsity assumption by a graph. Consider a directed graph <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{1,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{1,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of vertices, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of directed edges over <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Vertexes of the graph correspond to the sequence elements, and the directed edges are present if we assume a nonzero attention value for a general input. For each pair <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)\in V\times V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)\in V\times V</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following matrix element:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{nm}=\sigma(q_{n},k_{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{nm}=\sigma(q_{n},k_{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{if }(n,m)\in E," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{if }(n,m)\in E,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{ otherwise }0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>otherwise </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{ otherwise }0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">If the graph has structured sparsity, it can be used to reduce the computation cost. A simple example is a pattern of edges present in sliding windows <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\{(n,m):~|n-m|\leq T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{(n,m):~|n-m|\leq T\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some constant <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. The kernel matrix <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> will be a band matrix, with block size <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore the computational and memory complexity will be <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(TL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(TL)</annotation></semantics></math>. We illustrate some sparsity patterns and the corresponding attention matrices in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Sparsity assumptions ‣ 2.2 Attention Layer ‣ 2 Background ‣ Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S2.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:82.8pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel" height="69.45" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 77.12 69.45" width="77.12"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,69.45) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(-1.9,0) translate(0,79.02)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.25 0.0 0.0 0.25 3.11 -16.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:14.4pt;" height="12.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.84)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="1.440em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fill-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fg-color:#8080FF;" color="#8080FF" fill="#8080FF" stroke="#8080FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 9.84 -9.84 M 9.84 -9.84 L 9.84 -19.69 L 19.69 -19.69 L 19.69 -9.84 Z M 19.69 -19.69"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 9.84 -9.84 M 9.84 -9.84 L 9.84 -19.69 L 19.69 -19.69 L 19.69 -9.84 Z M 19.69 -19.69"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.25 0.0 0.0 0.25 3.11 -16.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:14.4pt;" height="12.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.84)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="1.440em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fill-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fg-color:#8080FF;" color="#8080FF" fill="#8080FF" stroke="#8080FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 19.69 -9.84 M 19.69 -9.84 L 19.69 -19.69 L 29.53 -19.69 L 29.53 -9.84 Z M 29.53 -19.69"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 19.69 -9.84 M 19.69 -9.84 L 19.69 -19.69 L 29.53 -19.69 L 29.53 -9.84 Z M 29.53 -19.69"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.25 0.0 0.0 0.25 3.11 -16.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:14.4pt;" height="12.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.84)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="1.440em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fill-color:#8080FF;--ltx-fg-color:#8080FF;" color="#8080FF" fill="#8080FF" stroke="#8080FF"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 29.53 -9.84 M 29.53 -9.84 L 29.53 -19.69 L 39.37 -19.69 L 39.37 -9.84 Z M 39.37 -19.69"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 29.53 -9.84 M 29.53 -9.84 L 29.53 -19.69 L 39.37 -19.69 L 39.37 -9.84 Z M 39.37 -19.69"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.25 0.0 0.0 0.25 3.11 -16.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:14.4pt;" height="12.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 12.84)" width="9.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m4"