</div></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" stroke="#000000"><path style="fill:none" d="M 182.25 -167.46 h 202.74 v 202.74 h -202.74 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 186.86 -66.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.99em;--ltx-fo-height:0em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="0" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)" width="193.58"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.F2.pic1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:13.99em;">
<span id="S3.F2.pic1.8.1" class="ltx_p"></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Correspondence between layer representation as a tree structure and block matrix representation. For element <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> red arrows highlight a tuple <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A_{4},C_{4},B_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A_{4},C_{4},B_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph node and its places in a matrix.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Identifying a gap</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we can see in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Sparsity assumptions ‣ 2.2 Attention Layer ‣ 2 Background ‣ Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, there is a wide variety of sparsity patterns, possibly reflecting domain knowledge. Although this is an efficient way to reduce computational cost, hard-zeroing out elements is a restrictive choice. In contrast, SSM-like layers invoke separability, which keeps the attention matrix dense, yet has an efficient way of computation via recurrence. Intuitively, it is clear that the recurrence in <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 State Space Model (SSM) Layer ‣ 2 Background ‣ Myosotis: structured computation for attention like layer" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> corresponds to the chain graph. Motivated by this, we make the connection precise and propose a Myosotis<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The forget-me-not flower’s scientific name: Myosotis</span></span></span> layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The sketch of the approach is the following. We construct a tree graph <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> from a sequence and consider the corresponding block tree matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The output of the proposed layer is the application of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the input <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. As the matrix is tree-structured (block), therefore solving <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}x=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}x=u</annotation></semantics></math> is an efficient recurrence-like computation. Hence, we fuse both ideas discussed above: we use the sparse <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, but apply the dense <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{G}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{G}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we discuss the algorithm in detail.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Myo Layer: Structure</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For explanation purposes in this section, we will construct the matrix associated with the layer explicitly; however, as we discuss further application of the layer, it is matrix-free. We parameterize the layer with a rooted tree graph <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider a (reversed) breadth-first search traverse (BFS) of the graph <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> levels, where the bottom (leaf) level is the first level and the root node is the last. The number of nodes at each level is denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{k},k\in\{1,\dots,D\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{k},k\in\{1,\dots,D\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\sum_{k=1}^{D}l_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\sum_{k=1}^{D}l_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. We label the leaf vertices from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the BFS order. The non-leaf node is labeled by its subtree with segment <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n,m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n,m]</annotation></semantics></math> of the most left leaf <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and the most right leaf <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, the root label is labeled <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1;l_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1;l_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> as it covers all leaves.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Layer input and output</h4>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:35%;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
